fix:
If ownership is always needed, accept the owned type directly (`String`, `Vec<T>`) or an appropriate consuming conversion such as `impl Into<String>`, so callers who already own the data can move it without allocation. If ownership is conditional, keep borrowing and allocate only in the branch that needs it, optionally using `Cow` with a lifetime suited to the actual storage requirement; note that `Cow` does not remove the allocation when the final representation must always own the input.
```rust
pub struct DBClient {
    connection_string: String,
}

impl DBClient {
    pub fn new(conn: String) -> Self {
        Self { connection_string: conn }
    }
}
```
